WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:01.584
OK, rolling...

00:00:01.584 --> 00:00:02.669
Test, test, one, two.. 

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:10.343
My name is Anna Zayaruznaya
 and I'm a music historian.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:13.763
Music is the most ephemeral thing. 

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:15.515
Music gets older faster
 than anything else.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:20.687
There's a guy Johannes Tinctoris,
 who said in the 1460s

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:25.400
that music older than 40 years
 isn't worth listening to. In the 1460s!

00:00:25.775 --> 00:00:28.236
And if you think about the music your parents listened to,

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:29.320
 maybe that's not your music.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.157
Music is so tied to the moment
 in which it's written

00:00:32.157 --> 00:00:34.492
and first performed.

00:00:34.492 --> 00:00:39.122
Given that, isn't it crazy we have music surviving from the Middle Ages?

00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:42.709
I study music history 

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:45.962
because it gives us a way
 to get at aspects of the past

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:50.258
that I don't think are available to us
 through any other means.

00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:54.345
There's something about music
 that is linked to the emotions,

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:56.222
that's linked to the body,
 that's linked to the voices that sing it

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:57.515
or the instruments that play it. 

00:00:57.515 --> 00:00:59.601
There's something
 so personal embodied about music.

00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:10.779
If you hear that music then,
and it sounds so weird

00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:13.073
for me that's the moment that's interesting, right,

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:17.535
is to then keep listening and say,
 OK, well, I think that sounds weird.

00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:19.287
But clearly they thought it was awesome. 

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:23.666
So what kind of a subject would I need
 to be to think that this is awesome

00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:26.419
and what kinds of insights, then,
 can I gain into

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:28.588
what it means to be human, what it means to be a listener? 

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.305
Often music manuscripts
 would get chopped up

00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:40.016
and they would get used
 to reinforce other bindings,

00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:42.977
so you'd have a book from the 17th century
 or something that would have

00:01:42.977 --> 00:01:46.439
that would have as part of its binding material,
 a piece of music from the 13th century.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:50.318
And you have to figure out what's been
 cut off in terms of words and notes and

00:01:50.318 --> 00:01:52.904
piece it all together.

00:01:54.864 --> 00:01:57.033
There are a couple of pieces of music
 that I've reconstructed

00:01:57.033 --> 00:02:00.829
from the 14th century
 that once I was done with them,

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:03.206
we could sing them in ways
 that we had never sung them before.

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:12.006
I think that's sort of what it
comes down to for me is to find

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:15.135
this music that feels foreboding
 because it's in a different notation,

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:17.011
because it's in these sources
 that are in these European archives

00:02:17.011 --> 00:02:18.388
it's full of wormholes, 

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:20.306
you know, it's decaying,
 the pages are falling off. 

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:21.933
It can be a mess. 

00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:26.896
And to restore it in some sense,
 to turn it into something that can tell us

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:28.898
something about a person who lived once 

00:02:28.898 --> 00:02:30.608
and how they might
 have experienced the world. 

00:02:34.529 --> 00:02:36.614
What is this music connected to? 

00:02:36.614 --> 00:02:38.616
Is it just a floating song? 

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:42.787
No, the music is always for somebody,
 from somebody, for some purpose.

00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:46.082
So what was its purpose? That's one of the things we're doing.

00:02:46.082 --> 00:02:49.502
But also it's a question
 of what we can learn about ourselves.

00:02:59.304 --> 00:03:02.140
I hope that my students come out of my classes 

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.685
inspired to listen more carefully,

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:08.771
especially when they hear something
 that they don't like.

00:03:08.771 --> 00:03:11.316
I never ask anybody
 to like the things we study.

00:03:11.316 --> 00:03:14.194
I just ask them to sort of
 attend to them carefully.

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:18.531
And I think the act of attending
carefully to something that sounds strange

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:20.074
is a life skill.

